# Safety model

<p class="lede">
ppd runs next to your real Paseo, and often inside a coding agent that Paseo itself is running. One wrong port, home
or environment variable would restart or rewrite the daemon you work in. These guards are code with tests, not
advice.
</p>

<h2 id="guards">The guards</h2>

| Guard | What it stops |
| --- | --- |
| Port 6767 is refused | `daemon up --port` and `android --port` refuse it, the automatic pick (7800 to 7899) skips it, and a daemon record pointing at it is not used. Ports below 1024 and anything that is not plain digits are refused too: `+6767` is not digits and `06767` parses to 6767, so neither slips through. |
| Homes live in temp directories | A dev home is a fresh `mkdtemp` directory named `ppd-<name>-` under `$TMPDIR`. After resolving symlinks it must sit strictly inside a temp root (`$TMPDIR`, `/tmp`, `/private/tmp`) and must not overlap `~/.paseo`, or `PASEO_HOME` when it points outside temp. |
| Only marked homes are deleted | Every home ppd creates gets a `.ppd-home` marker. `daemon down`, and the cleanup of a daemon that died, remove a home only when it carries the marker and passes the home check again. |
| Only its own processes are signalled | `daemon down` signals a pid only when that process's command line carries `--home <that dev home>`. Otherwise: `pid N is alive but is not this dev daemon; not signalling it`. |
| A scrubbed environment | The daemon, every Paseo CLI call aimed at a dev daemon and the plugin code `ppd check` runs get an environment without inherited `PASEO_*` variables (`PASEO_HOME`, `PASEO_AGENT_ID`, `PASEO_CLI`, ...; the daemon keeps only `PASEO_NODE_ENV`), so ppd run by an agent inside Paseo cannot reach the parent daemon. The daemon and the CLI calls then get `PASEO_HOME` set to the dev home. |
| Pinned CLI target | Every Paseo CLI call aimed at a dev daemon ends with `--home <dev home>` (only `paseo --version` and `paseo plugin init` run without one; they talk to no daemon). `ppd paseo -- ... --home X` or `--host X` is refused: `do not pass --home: ppd always targets its own dev daemon`. |
| Loopback only | The daemon listens on `127.0.0.1`, with relay and MCP off. A daemon record whose URL is not `http://127.0.0.1:<checked port>` is refused. |
| Real commands only in dev daemons | The fake agent runs `bash` steps with `run: true` only when the daemon's home has the `.ppd-home` marker. Installed into any other daemon, it refuses. See [Fake agent](/docs/fake-agent#bash). |
| Phones are read-only | `ppd android` never sends input or key events and never changes settings, and never guesses between several devices. See [Testing on a phone](/docs/android). |
| Public actions are opt-in | `ppd release` asks before it pushes and refuses without a terminal unless `--yes`. `ppd registry update` prints the push and PR commands unless `--yes`. `ppd registry submit` only prints the form URL. |

The guards live in [`src/safety.ts`](https://github.com/nkwib/paseo-plugin-devkit/blob/main/src/safety.ts) and
are pinned by `test/safety.test.ts` and `test/review-devkit-safety.test.ts`, which try bypasses such as symlinked
temp paths, relative paths into `~/.paseo`, port spellings and forged daemon records.

<h2 id="exit-3">Refusals exit with code 3</h2>

A refusal prints `refused:` and exits 3, so scripts and agents can tell it from an ordinary failure (exit 1):

```sh
npx ppd android up --port 6767; echo "exit $?"
```

```text
refused: refusing port 6767: it belongs to your real Paseo daemon
exit 3
```

The CI smoke test asserts the same for `ppd daemon up --port 6767`.

<h2 id="state">Where ppd keeps state</h2>

Daemon records (name, pid, port, home, URL) are JSON files in `$XDG_STATE_HOME/paseo-plugin-devkit/daemons`, by
default `~/.local/state/paseo-plugin-devkit/daemons`; set `PPD_STATE_DIR` to move them. Nothing is written under
`~/.paseo`. The daemon's own files (`config.json`, `daemon.out` and `daemon.log`, agents, projects) stay in its temp
home and go away with `ppd daemon down` (`--keep-home` keeps them for inspection).

<Callout kind="warn" title="Isolated from your Paseo, not from your machine">

A dev daemon is a real Paseo daemon running as your user. Plugin server code, and fake agent shell steps, can do
anything you can do on this machine. The guards keep ppd from touching your real Paseo; they do not sandbox the
plugin you are testing. Test plugins you trust, or run the whole thing in a VM or container.

</Callout>
